%S A402383 9,88,877,8766,87655,876544,8765433,87654322,876543211,8765432100,
%T A402383 87654320000,876543111111,8765431111100,87654311110000,
%U A402383 876543111000000,8765431100000000,87654310000000000,876543000000000000,8765422222222222222,87654222222222222211,876542222222222222100,8765422222222222220000
%N A402383 a(n) is the largest n-digit number from which no earlier term can be obtained by deleting digits and rearranging the rest.
%C A402383 An n-digit number is a positive integer with exactly n decimal digits (no leading zero); 0 is not a term. A number u can be obtained from m by deleting digits and rearranging the rest if some digits of m, in any positions, can be deleted and the remaining digits put in some order (possibly the order they are in) that spells u. For u shorter than m (the only case that the rule needs), u can be obtained from m if and only if every digit occurs in m at least as often as in u; for example, 877 cannot be obtained from 8766, which has only one 7. The sequence stops at the first n for which an earlier term can be obtained from every n-digit number; this then also holds for every larger n (proved in the notes file).
%C A402383 The sequence is finite: it has exactly A(10,0) - 2 terms (proved in the notes file), where A(m,n) is the Ackermann function, defined by A(0,n) = n+1, A(m+1,0) = A(m,1) and A(m+1,n+1) = A(m,A(m+1,n)); A126333 lists the numbers A(n,0), which are 1, 2, 3, 5, 13, 65533 for n = 0, ..., 5. In base b >= 2 the same rule gives exactly A(b,0) - 2 terms (also proved in the notes file): 1, 3, 11 and 65531 terms for b = 2, 3, 4 and 5, and a tower of 65536 2's, minus 5, for b = 6. Each term follows from the one before by a short recipe on its digits (see the Formula section; the Example section shows how the recipe makes the sequence long).
%C A402383 Only the number c_d of copies of each digit d matters: u can be obtained from a longer number m exactly when the monomial of u divides that of m, where the monomial of a number is the product of the powers X_d^(c_d). In this form the rule is the greedy construction of Moreno Socías for monomials of degrees 1, 2, 3, ... (1992, Section 4; see also León Sánchez and Ovchinnikov, 2016, equations (3.8)-(3.10)): at each degree it takes, among the monomials not divisible by an earlier one, that with the largest vector of exponents (c_9, ..., c_0), compared first by c_9, then by c_8, and so on. In b variables that construction takes one monomial of each degree 1, 2, ..., A(b,0) - 1 and then finds none of the next degree (1992, proof of Corollary 7.5); the last of these monomials, a power of X_0, would be a string of 0's, so the sequence has one term less.
%C A402383 This sequence has the greatest possible length among all lists y_1, y_2, ..., y_k of positive integers in which y_i has i digits and, for i < j, y_i cannot be obtained from y_j by deleting digits and rearranging the rest: no such list has more than A(10,0) - 2 terms. This follows from a bound of Moreno Socías (1991, Proposition 1.1), as restated by León Sánchez and Ovchinnikov (2016, Proposition 2.3); the notes file gives the derivation and a second, self-contained proof. The same holds for A402384 (the same rule with "smallest": 1, 20, 222, 2233, ...), whose n-th term is obtained from a(n) by a substitution of digits that keeps their places (see A402384).
%C A402383 The statement "for every base b >= 2 the rule in base b stops" is true, but it cannot be proved in first-order primitive recursive arithmetic, a formal system that has the primitive recursive functions (those built up from 0 and x+1 by composition and by recursion on one argument, such as sums, products and powers) and the usual quantifiers, but allows induction only for statements without quantifiers. By a theorem of Parsons (1970; see the Buss and Feferman links), a proof would give a primitive recursive function f with f(b) >= A(b,0) - 2 for all b >= 2, and there is none (derived in the notes file from Moreno Socías, 1992, proof of Corollary 7.5). For each single base, including 10, this system does prove that the rule stops (also proved in the notes file).
%C A402383 With "deleting digits" alone (the remaining digits keep their order) the rule gives A402229, which has the same first ten terms; for 11 <= n <= 30, A402229(n) > a(n) (see the Example section for n = 11). For the number of terms of A402229 only bounds are known (see A402229); by the lower bound given there, A402229 has more terms than this sequence. A197082 is an analog for divisibility of integers, with exponents of primes in place of digit counts. The formulation in digits, the recipe for the next term in digits and the correspondence with A402384 were not found in the OEIS or in the literature consulted. See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
