%F A402383 Let N = A(10,0) - 2 be the number of terms. Let H_1(t) = 2^t and, for r >= 2, let H_r(t) be the result of applying H_(r-1) t times, starting from 1; so H_2(t) = A014221(t) is a tower of t 2's (H_2(3) = 2^(2^2) = 16 and H_2(4) = 2^16 = 65536), and H_r(t) is 2 followed by r up-arrows and t in Knuth's notation. Then A(b,0) - 2 = H_(b-2)(3) - 5 for b >= 3; in particular N = H_8(3) - 5 = H_7(4) - 5. All statements of this section are proved in the notes file.
%F A402383 a(n+1) is obtained from a(n) as follows: let d be the smallest nonzero digit of a(n) and z the number of its 0's; delete one digit d and all the 0's, and append z+2 digits d-1. For example, 8765432100 (d = 1, z = 2) gives 87654320000, which (d = 2, z = 4) gives 876543111111. This holds for every n < N; applied to the last term a(N) = 10^(N-1), the recipe would give a string of 0's only. In every base b >= 2 the same recipe holds, and the last term is 1 followed by A(b,0) - 3 zeros.
%F A402383 The digits of a(n) are nonincreasing from left to right (so a(n) is a term of A009996).
%F A402383 a(19) is 87654 followed by fourteen 2's. For 1 <= i <= 14 and 0 <= j <= s, where s = 2^(i+1) - 2, a(19 - i + s + j) is 87654 followed by 14-i 2's, s-j 1's and 2j 0's; these indices are exactly 20, ..., 65537, each once (i = 1 gives n = 20, 21, 22, and i = 2 gives n = 23, ..., 29). Thus a(65537) is 87654 followed by 65532 0's; the next term, a(65538), is 8765 followed by 65534 3's.
%F A402383 a(n) begins with 876543 exactly for 7 <= n <= 18, with 87654 exactly for 6 <= n <= 65537, with 8765 exactly for 5 <= n <= H_2(65536), and with 8 exactly for 2 <= n <= A(9,0) = H_7(3) - 3.
%F A402383 a(n) + A402384(n) = 11*10^(n-1) - 1 exactly when the first digit of a(n) occurs only once in a(n); this is the case in particular for n = 1 and for 3 <= n <= A(9,0), but not for n = 2 (88 + 20 = 108).
%e A402383 a(1) = 9, the largest 1-digit number. a(2) = 88: each of 89, 90, ..., 99 contains a 9, so 9 could be obtained from it, while 88 has no 9.
%e A402383 a(3) = 877: no earlier term can be obtained from a 3-digit number exactly when it has no 9 and at most one 8, and 877 is the largest such number.
%e A402383 a(4) = 8766: no earlier term can be obtained from a 4-digit number exactly when it has no 9, at most one 8 and, if it has an 8, at most one 7 (otherwise 877 could be obtained from it); the largest such number is 8766.
%e A402383 a(11) = 87654320000, whereas A402229(11) = 87654320010: a(10) = 8765432100 can be obtained from 87654320010 by deleting a 0 and rearranging the rest, but not by deleting digits alone, since only one 0 stands after the 1.
%e A402383 For n = 8, ..., 18, a(n) is 876543 followed by c_2 digits 2, c_1 digits 1 and c_0 digits 0:
%e A402383    n  (c_2,c_1,c_0)  a(n)
%e A402383    8  (2,0,0)        87654322
%e A402383    9  (1,2,0)        876543211
%e A402383   10  (1,1,2)        8765432100
%e A402383   11  (1,0,4)        87654320000
%e A402383   12  (0,6,0)        876543111111
%e A402383   13  (0,5,2)        8765431111100
%e A402383   14  (0,4,4)        87654311110000
%e A402383   15  (0,3,6)        876543111000000
%e A402383   16  (0,2,8)        8765431100000000
%e A402383   17  (0,1,10)       87654310000000000
%e A402383   18  (0,0,12)       876543000000000000
%e A402383 Each row follows from the row above by the recipe of the Formula section; for example, from n = 12 to n = 13 (d = 1, z = 0) one 1 is deleted and two 0's are appended. The rows show why the sequence is so long: a 1 is exchanged for two 0's, and a 2 together with all z 0's for z+2 digits 1, so that using up one 2 takes the number of 0's from z to 2z+4 (from 0 at n = 8 to 4 at n = 11, and on to 12 at n = 18). Using up one 3 repeats this for each of the z+2 digits 2 that replace it (from 12 zeros at n = 18 to 65532 zeros at n = 65537), and each higher digit in turn repeats the step of the digit below it.
%e A402383 From a number that consists of 876543 followed by digits 2, 1 and 0, none of a(1), ..., a(7) can be obtained (it has no 9 and only one digit each of 8, 7, ..., 3), and an earlier term of the table can be obtained exactly when the number has at least as many 2's, 1's and 0's as that term. Each triple (c_2,c_1,c_0) in the table is, among the triples of nonnegative integers with sum n-6 that are not >= the triple of an earlier row in every coordinate, the largest one when triples are compared first by c_2 and then by c_1. Every triple with sum 13 = 19-6 is >= one of the eleven in every coordinate; this is why a(19) no longer begins with 876543.
%e A402383 In base 3 the same rule gives 2, 11, 100 (written in base 3) and stops: a 4-digit base-3 number has a 2, or two 1's, or is 1000, so that 2, 11 or 100 can be obtained from it.
%e A402383 In base 4 it gives the 11 terms 3, 22, 211, 2100, 20000, 111111, 1111100, 11110000, 111000000, 1100000000, 10000000000 (written in base 4), each obtained from the one before by the recipe.
%e A402383 In base 5 the first digit of the n-th term is 4 for n = 1, 3 for 2 <= n <= 13, 2 for 14 <= n <= 32765 and 1 for 32766 <= n <= 65531 (proved in the notes file), and the last term is 1 followed by 65530 zeros.
%o A402383 (Python)
%o A402383 # Definition-based search of disjoint boxes of free digit-count vectors.
%o A402383 B = [([0]*j+[1]+[0]*(9-j), [0]*j+[500]*(10-j)) for j in range(9)]
%o A402383 for n in range(1, 23):
%o A402383     candidates = []
%o A402383     for lo, hi in B:
%o A402383         if sum(lo) <= n <= sum(hi):
%o A402383             v, left = lo[:], n-sum(lo)
%o A402383             for i in range(10):
%o A402383                 t = min(left, hi[i]-v[i]); v[i] += t; left -= t
%o A402383             candidates.append(v)
%o A402383     if not candidates: break
%o A402383     v = max(candidates)
%o A402383     print(''.join(str(9-i)*c for i, c in enumerate(v)))
%o A402383     C = []
%o A402383     for lo, hi in B:
%o A402383         if any(hi[i] < v[i] for i in range(10)): C.append((lo, hi)); continue
%o A402383         lo = lo[:]
%o A402383         for i in range(10):
%o A402383             if lo[i] < v[i]:
%o A402383                 h = hi[:]; h[i] = v[i]-1; C.append((lo[:], h)); lo[i] = v[i]
%o A402383     B = [(lo, hi) for lo, hi in C if sum(hi) > n]
